{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"facts","component":"Facts","items":[{"label":"Rock type","value":"Extrusive igneous"},{"label":"Typical color","value":"Dark gray to black"},{"label":"Main minerals","value":"Plagioclase, pyroxene, olivine"},{"label":"Density","value":"~2.9–3.0 g/cm³"}]}}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"lead","component":"Prose","text":"**Basalt** is the most common volcanic rock on Earth, forming the ocean floor and many continental surfaces. It originates from rapid cooling of lava during [[volcanic eruption]] and solidifies into a fine‑grained matrix. The rock’s high iron and magnesium content gives it a characteristic dark hue, while its low silica makes it less viscous than rhyolitic lavas. Basaltic flows can travel great distances, shaping landscapes such as the Hawaiian shield volcanoes and the Columbia River basalt province."}]}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"s1h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Formation"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"s1p","component":"Prose","text":"Basalt forms when magma derived from the mantle rises through fissures or vents and reaches the surface as [[lava flow]]. The low viscosity of this magma allows it to spread out in thin sheets, cooling quickly to produce a fine‑grained texture. Crystallization of plagioclase feldspar and pyroxene dominates the mineralogy, while occasional olivine crystals indicate a relatively primitive magma source. Rapid cooling can also generate vesicular structures known as pumice when gases escape, though basaltic pumice is rarer than its rhyolitic counterpart."}]}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"s2h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Uses"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"s2p","component":"Prose","text":"Because of its durability and abundance, basalt is widely employed in construction and engineering. Crushed basalt aggregate is a key component of [[road pavement]], providing high load‑bearing capacity and resistance to wear. Its high thermal mass makes it suitable for building blocks that moderate indoor temperature fluctuations. In some regions, basalt fiber is extracted for reinforcement in composite materials, offering strength comparable to steel while being lighter and corrosion‑resistant. Additionally, basalt's low permeability makes it an effective barrier in landfill liners and water treatment systems."}]}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"s3h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Occurrence"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"s3p","component":"Prose","text":"Basalt covers roughly 70 % of the Earth's surface, forming the oceanic crust and extensive plateaus such as the Deccan Traps. It is prevalent at divergent plate boundaries where seafloor spreading creates new basaltic crust. Continental basaltic provinces occur in flood basalt events, often linked to mantle plumes like the one beneath the [[Icelandic hotspot]]. Smaller outcrops appear as volcanic islands, lava tubes, and basalt columns, the latter famously exemplified by the Giant's Causeway."}]}}

{"id":"root","component":"Column","children":["title","facts","lead","s1h","s1p","s2h","s2p","s3h","s3p"]} 
{"id":"title","component":"Heading","level":1,"text":"Basalt"} 
{"id":"facts","component":"Facts","items":[{"label":"Type","value":"Extrusive igneous rock"},{"label":"Primary minerals","value":"Plagioclase feldspar, pyroxene, olivine"},{"label":"Typical color","value":"Dark gray to black"},{"label":"Density","value":"~2.9–3.0 g/cm³"},{"label":"Common locations","value":"Oceanic crust, flood basalts"}]} 
{"id":"lead","component":"Prose","text":"**Basalt** is a fine‑grained, dark volcanic rock that forms the majority of the ocean floor and underlies many continental plateaus. It originates from rapid cooling of magma at the surface, producing a dense, durable material that shapes planetary geology."} 
{"id":"s1h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Formation"} 
{"id":"s1p","component":"Prose","text":"Basalt forms when mafic magma erupts during a [[Volcanic eruption]] and spreads as a thin [[Lava flow]] across the landscape. The high temperature and low silica content cause the melt to remain fluid, allowing it to travel great distances before solidifying. As the lava cools, crystals of plagioclase and pyroxene develop, giving basalt its characteristic fine texture. This process is integral to [[Plate tectonics]], where seafloor spreading creates new basaltic crust at mid‑ocean ridges."} 
{"id":"s2h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Properties"} 
{"id":"s2p","component":"Prose","text":"Basalt is noted for its high density and low porosity, making it resistant to weathering and erosion. Its mineral assemblage imparts a dark hue, though variations in iron and magnesium can produce subtle color differences. The rock exhibits a cleavage that is generally absent, but fractures can develop columnar joints reminiscent of those seen in [[Obsidian]] formations. These physical traits contribute to basalt's strength, which is comparable to that of engineered concrete, and its ability to retain heat, influencing local microclimates."} 
{"id":"s3h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Uses"} 
{"id":"s3p","component":"Prose","text":"Throughout history, basalt has been employed as a building material, especially in regions where it is abundant, such as the ancient [[Megalithic construction]] sites of the Mediterranean. Its durability makes it suitable for paving stones, retaining walls, and architectural veneers. Modern applications exploit its thermal mass for energy‑efficient construction, while crushed basalt serves as aggregate in road surfacing. Additionally, basalt fibers are manufactured for composite materials, offering a lightweight, fire‑resistant alternative to glass fibers in automotive and aerospace industries."}

