{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"root","component":"Column","children":["title","facts","lead","s1h","s1p","s2h","s2p","s3h","s3p"]}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"title","component":"Heading","level":1,"text":"Antikythera Mechanism"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"facts","component":"Facts","items":[{"label":"Date of discovery","value":"1901"},{"label":"Origin","value":"Ancient Greece"},{"label":"Purpose","value":"Astronomical calculator"},{"label":"Material","value":"Bronze"}]}}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"lead","component":"Prose","text":"The **Antikythera mechanism** is the earliest known analogue computer, recovered from a shipwreck off the island of Antikythera and reconstructed through modern imaging techniques. Its intricate gear train models the motions of celestial bodies with remarkable precision."}]}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"s1h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Historical context"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"s1p","component":"Prose","text":"The device dates to the Hellenistic period, a time of intense scientific activity under the patronage of the Ptolemaic dynasty. It reflects the engineering expertise of workshops that produced complex automata, such as the famed [[Hero of Alexandria]] devices, and underscores the diffusion of astronomical knowledge across the Mediterranean."}]}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"s2h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Construction and function"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"s2p","component":"Prose","text":"The mechanism comprises a front dial displaying the zodiac and a rear dial tracking the Metonic cycle. A series of bronze gears encode the synodic month, the eclipse prediction algorithm, and the irregularities of the lunar orbit. Modern computed tomography revealed over 30 interlocking gears, a testament to the sophisticated craftsmanship of ancient metalworkers."}]}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"s3h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Legacy and research"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"s3p","component":"Prose","text":"Since its discovery, the Antikythera mechanism has inspired interdisciplinary study, merging archaeology, physics, and computer science. It has reshaped our understanding of ancient technology, demonstrating that concepts now associated with the Renaissance were already in practice centuries earlier, and it continues to influence contemporary designs of mechanical computing devices."}]}

{"id":"root","component":"Column","children":["title","facts","lead","histH","histP","designH","designP","signifH","signifP"]}  
{"id":"title","component":"Heading","level":1,"text":"Antikythera Mechanism"}  
{"id":"facts","component":"Facts","items":[{"label":"Date of discovery","value":"1901"},{"label":"Location found","value":"Antikythera shipwreck, Greece"},{"label":"Estimated age","value":"c. 100 BC"},{"label":"Material","value":"Bronze"}]}  
{"id":"lead","component":"Prose","text":"The **Antikythera mechanism** is an ancient analog computer discovered among the wreckage of a Roman-era ship off the Greek island of Antikythera. Scholars have identified intricate gear trains that model celestial cycles, making it the earliest known example of a complex mechanical device. Its construction reflects the sophisticated engineering of [[Hellenistic science]], while the preservation of its corroded bronze parts provides insight into ancient manufacturing techniques. The find sparked interdisciplinary research linking archaeology, astronomy, and the history of technology."}  
{"id":"histH","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"History"}  
{"id":"histP","component":"Prose","text":"The mechanism was recovered in 1901 by sponge divers exploring a shipwreck dated to the first century AD. Initial examinations revealed a corroded lump of metal, but it was not until the early 2000s that advanced imaging uncovered its internal complexity. Researchers such as Derek de Bruijn and Michael Wolfram employed X‑ray tomography to reveal the gear arrangement, confirming that the device could predict lunar and solar eclipses. This breakthrough highlighted the transmission of astronomical knowledge from classical Greece to the Roman world."}  
{"id":"designH","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Design and Function"}  
{"id":"designP","component":"Prose","text":"The Antikythera mechanism comprises at least 30 interlocking bronze gears, each meticulously cut to precise ratios. The front dial displays the zodiac and the solar calendar, while a rear dial tracks the Metonic cycle, aligning lunar months with the solar year. Additional subsidiary dials indicate the positions of the known planets, a capability comparable to later astronomical clocks of the medieval period. The device's gear trains embody principles of mechanical engineering that would not reappear until the invention of complex clocks in the 14th century."}  
{"id":"signifH","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Significance"}  
{"id":"signifP","component":"Prose","text":"The existence of the Antikythera mechanism challenges the assumption that ancient societies lacked the capacity for sophisticated machinery. It demonstrates that Hellenistic scholars possessed not only theoretical knowledge of astronomy but also the practical ability to encode that knowledge in a portable, programmable instrument. The mechanism's discovery has reshaped our understanding of technological development, suggesting a lost tradition of complex devices that may have been destroyed or repurposed during the decline of the Roman Empire."}

