{"version":"1.0","createSurface":{"surfaceId":"page","catalogId":"latentspace"}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"root","component":"Column","children":["title","facts","lead","h1","p1","h2","p2","h3","p3","h4","p4"]}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"title","component":"Heading","level":1,"text":"Fermentation"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"facts","component":"Facts","items":[{"label":"Primary agents","value":"Yeast, bacteria, molds"},{"label":"Typical pH","value":"4.0–5.0"},{"label":"Key products","value":"Ethanol, lactic acid, carbon dioxide"},{"label":"Historical span","value":"Prehistoric to modern biotech"}]}}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"lead","component":"Prose","text":"**Fermentation** is a metabolic conversion of organic substrates into simpler compounds, harnessed by humans for food preservation, flavor development, and bio‑production. The process exploits the activity of microorganisms such as [[Yeast]] and [[Lactic acid bacteria]], and is exemplified in traditional foods like [[Kombucha]] and [[Kimchi]]."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"h1","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"History"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"p1","component":"Prose","text":"Evidence of fermented beverages and breads dates to the Neolithic era, when early agrarian societies discovered that grain mash left to rest produced alcohol and leavened dough. Ancient Mesopotamian tablets record recipes for barley wine, while Chinese records describe rice wine production as early as 3000 BCE. Throughout antiquity, fermentation spread across continents, becoming integral to cultural cuisines and religious rites."}]}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"h2","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Process"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"p2","component":"Prose","text":"The core of fermentation is the anaerobic breakdown of sugars by microbes, yielding energy and characteristic metabolites. In alcoholic fermentation, yeast convert glucose to ethanol and CO₂ via glycolysis and the pyruvate decarboxylase pathway. Lactic acid fermentation, performed by lactic acid bacteria, reduces pyruvate to lactic acid, lowering pH and inhibiting spoilage organisms. Variables such as temperature, substrate composition, and inoculum size dictate the rate and profile of the reaction, allowing precise control in industrial settings."}]}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"h3","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Applications"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"p3","component":"Prose","text":"Modern biotechnology exploits fermentation for large‑scale production of commodities ranging from ethanol fuel to antibiotics and enzymes. Food and beverage industries rely on controlled fermentations to create cheese, yogurt, sourdough, and craft beers. Emerging sectors apply the technique to bioplastics and protein alternatives, demonstrating its versatility as a sustainable manufacturing platform."}]}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"h4","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Health Effects"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"p4","component":"Prose","text":"Fermented foods often contain live cultures that can modulate gut microbiota, enhancing digestive health and immune function. The production of short‑chain fatty acids during lactic fermentation contributes to colonocyte nourishment and anti‑inflammatory pathways. However, excessive alcohol fermentation products may pose risks, underscoring the need for balanced consumption."}]}}

{"id":"root","component":"Column","children":["title","facts","lead","hist_h","hist_p","proc_h","proc_p","app_h","app_p"]} 
{"id":"title","component":"Heading","level":1,"text":"Fermentation"} 
{"id":"facts","component":"Facts","items":[{"label":"Definition","value":"Metabolic conversion of sugars into acids, gases, or alcohols by microorganisms"},{"label":"Primary agents","value":"Yeasts, bacteria, and sometimes molds"},{"label":"Typical temperature","value":"20–30 °C for most yeasts, lower for lactic acid bacteria"},{"label":"Common products","value":"Alcoholic beverages, dairy products, baked goods"}]} 
{"id":"lead","component":"Prose","text":"**Fermentation** is a biochemical process harnessed by humans for millennia, transforming organic substrates into flavorful and preservative compounds. The pioneering work of [[Louis Pasteur]] clarified its microbial nature, while the production of [[Ethanol]] underpins modern fuel and beverage industries. Fermented teas such as [[Kombucha]] illustrate its diverse culinary applications."} 
{"id":"hist_h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Historical development"} 
{"id":"hist_p","component":"Prose","text":"Evidence of fermentation dates to [[Ancient Egypt]], where grain beers were brewed for ritual and nutrition. The refinement of wine-making techniques in the [[Sherry]] region advanced understanding of controlled aging. Parallel traditions in East Asia produced fermented vegetables like [[Kimchi]], showcasing cultural adaptation of microbial preservation."} 
{"id":"proc_h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Biochemical process"} 
{"id":"proc_p","component":"Prose","text":"The process relies on microbial metabolism; [[Lactobacillus]] bacteria convert sugars to lactic acid, while [[Yeast]] species such as Saccharomyces cerevisiae generate ethanol and carbon dioxide. These reactions occur under [[Anaerobic respiration]] conditions, creating environments that inhibit spoilage organisms."} 
{"id":"app_h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Contemporary applications"} 
{"id":"app_p","component":"Prose","text":"Today, fermentation is central to [[Beer brewing]], where precise yeast strains define flavor profiles. In [[Bread making]], leavening gases expand dough, producing the familiar crumb structure. The rise of [[Probiotics]] highlights health benefits associated with live microbial cultures in foods and supplements."}

