{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"facts","component":"Facts","items":[{"label":"Capital","value":"Constantinople"},{"label":"Duration","value":"330–1453 CE"},{"label":"Language","value":"Greek"},{"label":"Religion","value":"Orthodox Christianity"},{"label":"Peak territory","value":"Eastern Mediterranean"}]}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"lead","component":"Prose","text":"The **Byzantine Empire** was the continuation of the Eastern Roman world, preserving classical heritage while forging a distinct medieval civilization. Its capital, [[Constantinople]], served as a hub of trade, culture, and strategic power, and its legal reforms under [[Justinian I]] shaped later jurisprudence."}]}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"s1h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"History"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"s1p","component":"Prose","text":"Founded by Emperor Constantine in 330 CE, the empire survived the fall of Rome and endured numerous challenges, including the iconoclastic controversy that divided clergy and state. The Macedonian dynasty later restored stability, while the empire's position along the [[Silk Road]] facilitated exchange between East and West."}]}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"s2h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Government and Law"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"s2p","component":"Prose","text":"The Byzantine state was an absolute monarchy with a complex bureaucracy. The emperor wielded both secular and religious authority, supported by the imperial court and the Senate. The Corpus Juris Civilis, compiled under Justinian, codified Roman law and influenced legal systems for centuries."}]}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"s3h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Culture and Legacy"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"s3p","component":"Prose","text":"Byzantine art blended classical realism with spiritual symbolism, exemplified by the mosaics of [[Hagia Sophia]]. Its theological writings preserved patristic thought, and its scholars transmitted Greek philosophy to the Islamic world and the Latin West, shaping the Renaissance."}]}

