{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"lead","component":"Prose","text":"The **Byzantine Empire** was the continuation of the Roman state in the eastern Mediterranean, preserving classical traditions while fostering a distinct medieval civilization. Its strategic position enabled the development of sophisticated diplomacy, and its legacy endures in art, law, and theology."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"history_h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Historical Development"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"history_p","component":"Prose","text":"Founded by Emperor Constantine I in 330 CE, the empire reached its zenith under [[Justinian I]], who codified Roman law and commissioned the monumental [[Hagia Sophia]]. The period of [[Iconoclasm]] in the 8th–9th centuries sparked theological conflict and artistic transformation, while the later Macedonian dynasty restored stability and cultural flourishing."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"culture_h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Culture and Religion"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"culture_p","component":"Prose","text":"Byzantine culture blended Greco‑Roman heritage with Christian theology, producing iconic mosaics and illuminated manuscripts. The establishment of [[Eastern Orthodoxy]] as the state faith shaped liturgical practice and ecclesiastical architecture, influencing neighboring Slavic lands. Monastic centers such as Mount Athos became hubs of spiritual scholarship and preservation of classical texts."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"economy_h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Economy and Trade"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"economy_p","component":"Prose","text":"The empire's economy thrived on a sophisticated monetary system and control of key trade routes linking Europe and Asia. Silk production, regulated by imperial monopoly, fueled commercial prosperity, while the strategic harbor of [[Constantinople]] facilitated exchange of goods, ideas, and diplomatic envoys across the Mediterranean and Black Sea."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"legacy_h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Legacy"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"legacy_p","component":"Prose","text":"The fall of Constantinople in 1453 marked the end of Byzantine political authority, but its legal codes, artistic motifs, and theological doctrines persisted. Scholars such as [[Theodosius II]] preserved ancient knowledge, and the empire's diplomatic practices influenced Renaissance humanism, leaving an indelible imprint on European civilization."}]}}

