{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"root","component":"Column","children":["title","facts","lead","s1h","s1p","s2h","s2p","s3h","s3p"]}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"title","component":"Heading","level":1,"text":"Basalt"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"facts","component":"Facts","items":[{"label":"Type","value":"Extrusive igneous rock"},{"label":"Composition","value":"Mafic / Ultramafic"},{"label":"Silica Content","value":"< 52%"},{"label":"Color","value":"Dark gray to black"},{"label":"Density","value":"2.9–3.0 g/cm³"}]}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"lead","component":"Prose","text":"**Basalt** is the most common extrusive igneous rock on Earth, forming the majority of the planet's oceanic crust and significant portions of its continental surface. It is a fine-grained, dark-colored rock formed from the rapid cooling of low-viscosity lava rich in iron and magnesium. Because of its abundance, basalt serves as a primary structural material in the [[Geology]] of both terrestrial and extraterrestrial environments."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"s1h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Formation and Texture"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"s1p","component":"Prose","text":"Basalt originates from the partial melting of the Earth's mantle, producing magma with a high temperature and low viscosity. When this magma reaches the surface, it cools quickly, resulting in a fine-grained or aphanitic texture where individual crystals are often microscopic. This rapid cooling prevents the growth of large mineral grains, distinguishing basalt from its slow-cooled intrusive counterpart, [[Gabbro]]."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"s2h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Mineral Composition"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"s2p","component":"Prose","text":"The mineral assemblage of basalt is dominated by plagioclase feldspar and pyroxene, often accompanied by olivine. The dark coloration is primarily due to the presence of mafic minerals like [[Magnetite]] and ilmenite. The specific ratio of these minerals can vary, leading to different subtypes such as tholeiitic or alkaline basalt, which have distinct geochemical signatures useful for tracing the origin of the magma source."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"s3h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Geological Significance"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"s3p","component":"Prose","text":"Basalt plays a critical role in the [[Plate Tectonics]] cycle, as it is the primary rock produced at mid-ocean ridges and hotspots. Large igneous provinces, such as the Deccan Traps, consist of massive basaltic flood lavas that have influenced global climate and biodiversity. Its durability and compressive strength also make it a preferred material for construction and road construction in many parts of the world."}]}}

{"id":"root","component":"Column","children":["title","facts","lead","s1h","s1p","s2h","s2p","s3h","s3p"]}
{"id":"title","component":"Heading","level":1,"text":"Basalt"}
{"id":"facts","component":"Facts","items":[{"label":"Classification","value":"Extrusive igneous"},{"label":"Texture","value":"Fine-grained"},{"label":"Color","value":"Dark grey to black"},{"label":"Primary Location","value":"Oceanic crust"},{"label":"Major Minerals","value":"Plagioclase, pyroxene"}]}
{"id":"lead","component":"Prose","text":"**Basalt** is the most common extrusive igneous rock on Earth, forming the vast majority of the planet's oceanic crust. It is characterized by its fine-grained texture and dark coloration, resulting from the rapid cooling of low-viscosity lava. This rock type is fundamental to understanding plate tectonics and the geological history of both the ocean floors and the continental surface."}
{"id":"s1h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Formation and Composition"}
{"id":"s1p","component":"Prose","text":"Basalt forms when magma, typically derived from the upper mantle, erupts onto the surface or shallow crust. The rapid cooling prevents large crystals from forming, creating a dense, fine-grained matrix. Chemically, it is mafic, meaning it is rich in magnesium and iron but poor in silica. The primary mineral components are plagioclase feldspar and pyroxene, often with minor amounts of olivine."}
{"id":"s2h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Geological Significance"}
{"id":"s2p","component":"Prose","text":"The majority of Earth's surface is composed of basaltic material. Mid-ocean ridges are sites of continuous basalt creation as tectonic plates diverge. On continents, basalt appears in large igneous provinces, such as the Columbia River Basalt Group. These formations provide critical data regarding paleomagnetism and the movement of tectonic plates over millions of years."}
{"id":"s3h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Physical Properties"}
{"id":"s3p","component":"Prose","text":"Basalt is typically dense, with a specific gravity ranging from 2.7 to 3.0. It is durable and resistant to weathering, making it a popular building stone throughout history. In some cases, basalt cools in columns, forming hexagonal structures known as columnar basalt, which are often found in volcanic necks and cliff faces."}

