{"version":"1.0","createSurface":{"surfaceId":"page","catalogId":"latentspace"}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"root","component":"Column","children":["title","facts","lead","s1h","s1p","s2h","s2p","s3h","s3p"]}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"title","component":"Heading","level":1,"text":"Antikythera mechanism"},{"id":"facts","component":"Facts","items":[{"label":"Date","value":"c. 150–100 BCE"},{"label":"Function","value":"Analog planetary computer"},{"label":"Location","value":"Antikythera, Greece"},{"label":"Components","value":"37+ bronze gears"},{"label":"Discovery","value":"1901"}]}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"lead","component":"Prose","text":"The **Antikythera mechanism** is the oldest known analog computer, a sophisticated Greek device used to predict astronomical positions and eclipses for calendrical and horological purposes. Discovered in a shipwreck off the island of [[Antikythera]] in 1901, the artifact demonstrates a level of mechanical complexity that was not seen again for over a thousand years, challenging previous assumptions about the technological capabilities of the [[Hellenistic period]]."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"s1h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Mechanical Design"},{"id":"s1p","component":"Prose","text":"The device consists of a complex system of bronze gears, including a differential gear mechanism that calculates the relative positions of the [[Sun]] and [[Moon]]. The primary input was a hand-cranked shaft that drove the entire gear train. The front dial displayed the zodiac with a pointer for the Moon, while the back dials tracked the 19-year [[Metonic cycle]] for lunar eclipses and the 223-lunar-month Saros cycle for solar eclipses. The precision of the gear ratios suggests knowledge of the elliptical orbit of the Moon, a concept later formalized by [[Ptolemy]]."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"s2h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Discovery and Analysis"},{"id":"s2p","component":"Prose","text":"For decades, the corroded remains were dismissed as a simple clock. It was not until [[Michael Wright]] and later the team led by [[Tony Freeth]] used advanced imaging techniques such as X-ray tomography to map the internal structure that the device’s true nature was revealed. In 2007, a high-resolution scan confirmed that the mechanism contained at least 37 gears, some with as many as 223 teeth, allowing for extremely precise calculations of celestial movements."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"s3h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Historical Significance"},{"id":"s3p","component":"Prose","text":"The existence of the Antikythera mechanism implies that there was a sophisticated tradition of mechanical engineering in the ancient world that was lost during the [[Roman Empire]]. Its discovery has led to the search for other similar devices, with a potential second mechanism found on the island of Giza. The device stands as a testament to the scientific inquiry of the [[Greeks]], bridging the gap between ancient astronomy and the development of the modern scientific method."}]}}

{"id":"root","component":"Column","children":["title","facts","lead","s1h","s1p","s2h","s2p","s3h","s3p"]}
{"id":"title","component":"Heading","level":1,"text":"Antikythera mechanism"}
{"id":"facts","component":"Facts","items":[{"label":"Discovery","value":"1901 by sponge divers"},{"label":"Location","value":"Antikythera shipwreck, Greece"},{"label":"Date","value":"c. 150–100 BCE"},{"label":"Function","value":"Astronomical calculator"},{"label":"Material","value":"Bronze and wood"},{"label":"Preservation","value":"Corroded fragmentary state"}]}
{"id":"lead","component":"Prose","text":"The **Antikythera mechanism** is an ancient Greek hand-powered mechanical computer used to predict astronomical positions and eclipses for calendrical and astrological purposes. Discovered in a shipwreck off the Greek island of Antikythera, it is the oldest known example of an analog computer. The device incorporates at least thirty meshing bronze gears and is of a complexity that was not matched in surviving devices until the development of medieval astronomical clocks."}
{"id":"s1h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Discovery and Condition"}
{"id":"s1p","component":"Prose","text":"The mechanism was recovered in 1901 by a team of Greek sponge divers exploring the Antikythera wreck. Initially cataloged as a lump of corroded bronze, it was not identified as a complex instrument until 1951, when British physicist [[Decker]] noted the gear teeth. The fragmentary state of the device has required extensive imaging and computational reconstruction to understand its internal architecture and operational range."}
{"id":"s2h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Internal Structure"}
{"id":"s2p","component":"Prose","text":"Recent micro-CT scans have revealed a sophisticated system of differential gears. The device features a differential gear that subtracts the [[Solar cycle]] from the [[Lunar cycle]] to track the Metonic cycle. This innovation allows the mechanism to model the irregular motion of the Moon and predict solar and lunar eclipses with high accuracy. The precision of the gearing suggests a level of engineering previously thought impossible for the Hellenistic period."}
{"id":"s3h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Historical Significance"}
{"id":"s3p","component":"Prose","text":"Scholars debate the specific workshop and date of manufacture, with estimates ranging from the late second century to the early first century BCE. The device represents a peak of [[Hellenistic science]] and likely has a lost lineage of similar instruments. Its discovery fundamentally altered the historical understanding of ancient technological capabilities, demonstrating that the ancient world possessed advanced knowledge of mechanics and astronomy."}

