{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"root","component":"Column","children":["title","facts","lead","s1h","s1p","s2h","s2p","s3h","s3p","s4h","s4p"]}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"title","component":"Heading","level":1,"text":"Fermentation"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"facts","component":"Facts","items":[{"label":"Type","value":"Metabolic process"},{"label":"Key Organisms","value":"[[Yeast]] and [[Bacteria]]"},{"label":"Energy Yield","value":"Low (2 ATP per glucose)"},{"label":"Primary Products","value":"Ethanol, Lactic Acid, CO2"},{"label":"Historical Use","value":"Food Preservation"}]}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"lead","component":"Prose","text":"**Fermentation** is a metabolic process that converts sugar to acids, gases, or alcohol in the absence of oxygen or other electron acceptors. It is carried out by [[Microorganisms]] such as [[Yeast]] and [[Bacteria]], and has been used by humans for millennia in [[Food Preservation]] and the production of [[Beverages]]."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"s1h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Metabolic Mechanisms"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"s1p","component":"Prose","text":"During fermentation, [[Glycolysis]] breaks down glucose into pyruvate, generating a small amount of [[ATP]]. Because oxygen is absent, pyruvate is converted into waste products to regenerate NAD+, allowing glycolysis to continue. In [[Alcoholic Fermentation]], pyruvate becomes [[Ethanol]] and carbon dioxide. In [[Lactic Acid Fermentation]], it becomes [[Lactic Acid]]. This process is less efficient than aerobic respiration but does not require oxygen."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"s2h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Historical Applications"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"s2p","component":"Prose","text":"Humans have utilized fermentation since the Neolithic era. The earliest known [[Bread]] making occurred in the [[Fertile Crescent]], while [[Beer]] production was central to [[Mesopotamian]] culture. [[Vinegar]] and fermented dairy products like [[Yogurt]] and [[Cheese]] are among the oldest preserved foods. These processes extended shelf life and enhanced nutritional value before the invention of [[Refrigeration]]."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"s3h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Modern Industrial Uses"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"s3p","component":"Prose","text":"In modern industry, fermentation is critical for producing [[Antibiotics]], [[Vaccines]], and [[Biofuels]]. [[Enzyme]] production for detergents and textiles relies on high-yield strains of [[E. coli]] and [[Aspergillus]]. The biotechnology sector uses [[Bioreactors]] to control temperature and pH, maximizing output for pharmaceutical and agricultural applications."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"s4h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Scientific Significance"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"s4p","component":"Prose","text":"The study of fermentation led to the discovery of [[Enzymes]] and the role of [[Vitalism]] in biology. Louis Pasteur’s experiments disproved spontaneous generation, proving that [[Microbes]] cause fermentation. This work laid the foundation for [[Microbiology]] and [[Pasteurization]], transforming food safety and medical science."}]}}

{"id":"root","component":"Column","children":["title","facts","lead","s1h","s1p","s2h","s2p","s3h","s3p"]}
{"id":"title","component":"Heading","level":1,"text":"Fermentation"}
{"id":"facts","component":"Facts","items":[{"label":"Definition","value":"Metabolic conversion of sugars to acids, gases, or alcohol"},{"label":"Primary Agents","value":"Yeasts and Bacteria"},{"label":"Discovery","value":"Observed in ancient brewing and baking"},{"label":"Industrial Scale","value":"Major sector of global biotechnology"},{"label":"Key Products","value":"Ethanol, Lactic Acid, Acetic Acid"}]}
{"id":"lead","component":"Prose","text":"**Fermentation** is the metabolic process by which microorganisms convert carbohydrates into alcohols, organic acids, or gases in the absence of oxygen. This biochemical pathway serves as a critical mechanism for cellular energy production and has been harnessed for thousands of years to preserve food and produce beverages. The process relies on enzymatic activity within [[Microorganism]] cells, transforming complex sugars into simpler compounds that define the flavor, texture, and safety of fermented products."}
{"id":"s1h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Biochemical Mechanisms"}
{"id":"s1p","component":"Prose","text":"The fundamental biology of fermentation involves [[Glycolysis]], the initial stage of sugar breakdown. In this anaerobic environment, glucose is converted into pyruvate, which is then reduced by enzymes to regenerate NAD+ for continued energy production. The specific outcome depends on the microbial species involved; for instance, [[Yeast]] converts pyruvate into ethanol and carbon dioxide, while [[Lactic acid bacteria]] produce lactic acid. This metabolic flexibility allows different organisms to thrive in diverse environments, from the human gut to industrial bioreactors."}
{"id":"s2h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Historical and Cultural Significance"}
{"id":"s2p","component":"Prose","text":"Humans have utilized fermentation techniques since the [[Neolithic period]], initially for accidental preservation of grain and milk. The deliberate control of fermentation led to the creation of staple foods such as bread, cheese, and soy sauce, as well as alcoholic beverages like beer and wine. These processes not only extended shelf life but also introduced complex flavor profiles and increased the bioavailability of nutrients. The cultural importance of fermentation is evident in the distinct culinary traditions of every major civilization, where specific strains of microbes are cultivated to achieve precise sensory outcomes."}
{"id":"s3h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Modern Industrial Applications"}
{"id":"s3p","component":"Prose","text":"In contemporary science, fermentation has evolved from a culinary art into a sophisticated [[Biotechnology]] tool. Industrial facilities use controlled bioreactors to produce large quantities of pharmaceuticals, biofuels, and chemical intermediates. [[Genetic engineering]] has enabled scientists to modify microbial strains for higher yields of specific compounds, such as insulin or [[Bioethanol]]. This sector is vital for sustainable manufacturing, offering a renewable alternative to petrochemical processes while reducing waste through the upcycling of agricultural byproducts into valuable biological products."}

