{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"facts","component":"Facts","items":[{"label":"Capital","value":"Constantinople"},{"label":"Founding","value":"330 AD"},{"label":"Fall","value":"1453 AD"},{"label":"Religion","value":"Eastern Orthodox Christianity"},{"label":"Language","value":"Greek"}]}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"lead","component":"Prose","text":"The **Byzantine Empire** was the eastern continuation of the Roman Empire, centered on Constantinople. It preserved classical learning, developed a distinct legal and artistic tradition, and served as a buffer between Europe and the Islamic world for over a millennium."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"s1h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Origins"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"s1p","component":"Prose","text":"Following the division of the Roman Empire in 395 AD, the East retained greater stability and wealth. Emperor Constantine I established Constantinople as the new capital in 330 AD, shifting the imperial center of gravity to the Balkans and the Aegean Sea. This move facilitated a gradual cultural shift from Latin to [[Greek]] as the primary language of administration and literature."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"s2h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Golden Age"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"s2p","component":"Prose","text":"The reign of Justinian I in the 6th century marked the empire's territorial zenith. Justinian's codification of Roman law, known as the [[Corpus Juris Civilis]], influenced legal systems across Europe for centuries. Under his rule, the Hagia Sophia was constructed, becoming an architectural masterpiece that symbolized imperial power and religious devotion. The period saw significant expansion into North Africa, Italy, and parts of Spain."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"s3h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Iconoclasm and Recovery"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"s3p","component":"Prose","text":"The 8th and 9th centuries were defined by the Iconoclast Controversy, a theological and political struggle over the use of religious images. After the restoration of icons in 843, the empire entered a period of cultural and military recovery. The Macedonian dynasty strengthened the military, expanded territory, and fostered a renaissance in literature and philosophy, often referred to as the [[Macedonian Renaissance]]."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"s4h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Decline and Fall"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"s4p","component":"Prose","text":"The Fourth Crusade in 1204 resulted in the sack of Constantinople by Western European forces, severely weakening the state. The empire fragmented into rival successor states before being re-established in 1261. Over the following centuries, the [[Ottoman Turks]] steadily conquered Byzantine territories. The final fall of Constantinople in 1453 to the Ottoman Sultan Mehmed II marked the end of the medieval period in Europe and the rise of the Ottoman Empire."}]}}

{"id":"root","component":"Column","children":["title","facts","lead","s1h","s1p","s2h","s2p","s3h","s3p"]}
{"id":"title","component":"Heading","level":1,"text":"Byzantine Empire"}
{"id":"facts","component":"Facts","items":[{"label":"Capital","value":"Constantinople"},{"label":"Founded","value":"330 AD"},{"label":"Dissolved","value":"1453 AD"},{"label":"Official Language","value":"Greek"},{"label":"Religion","value":"Eastern Orthodox Christianity"}]}
{"id":"lead","component":"Prose","text":"The **Byzantine Empire**, also known as the Eastern Roman Empire, was the continuation of the Roman Empire in its eastern provinces, centered on Constantinople. It served as a bridge between the classical and medieval worlds, preserving Roman law and Greek philosophy while developing a distinct Christian identity that influenced neighboring cultures for over a millennium."}
{"id":"s1h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Origins and Structure"}
{"id":"s1p","component":"Prose","text":"Following the division of the Roman Empire in 395 AD, the eastern half retained its administrative integrity and economic strength. The empire was governed by an autocratic [[Emperor]] who held supreme civil and religious authority. Its bureaucracy was highly organized, drawing on [[Roman Law]] to maintain order across diverse territories, including the Balkans, Anatolia, and parts of the Middle East."}
{"id":"s2h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Cultural and Religious Influence"}
{"id":"s2p","component":"Prose","text":"Byzantine culture was deeply intertwined with [[Eastern Orthodox Christianity]], which became the state religion and a central pillar of societal cohesion. The empire was a major center for art and architecture, exemplified by the Hagia Sophia, a masterpiece of engineering and spiritual symbolism. Byzantine scholars played a crucial role in preserving ancient Greek and Roman texts, transmitting this knowledge to the Islamic world and later to Western Europe during the Renaissance."}
{"id":"s3h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Decline and Fall"}
{"id":"s3p","component":"Prose","text":"The empire faced persistent external threats, particularly from the [[Ottoman Turks]] and various Slavic and Persian groups. Internal political instability and economic strain further weakened its defenses. The fall of Constantinople in 1453 to the Ottomans marked the end of the Byzantine state, though its cultural and legal legacies continued to shape the identity of Southeastern Europe and the broader Christian world."}

