{"id":"root","component":"Column","children":["title","facts","lead","formation_h","formation_p","composition_h","composition_p","occurrence_h","occurrence_p"]} 
{"id":"title","component":"Heading","level":1,"text":"Basalt"} 
{"id":"facts","component":"Facts","items":[{"label":"Rock type","value":"Igneous"},{"label":"Origin","value":"Volcanic"},{"label":"Typical color","value":"Dark gray"},{"label":"Main minerals","value":"Plagioclase, pyroxene, olivine"}]} 
{"id":"lead","component":"Prose","text":"**Basalt** is a fine‑grained, dark volcanic rock that forms the majority of the oceanic crust and many continental flood basalt provinces. It is produced when magma rich in [[mafic]] minerals erupts rapidly and cools at the surface."} 
{"id":"formation_h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Formation"} 
{"id":"formation_p","component":"Prose","text":"Basaltic magma originates deep in the mantle, often at divergent plate boundaries or hotspots. As the magma ascends, it undergoes limited differentiation, retaining high concentrations of iron and magnesium. When it reaches the surface, the rapid loss of heat causes the lava to solidify into a dense, glassy matrix."} 
{"id":"composition_h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Composition"} 
{"id":"composition_p","component":"Prose","text":"The primary mineral constituents of basalt are [[plagioclase feldspar]], [[pyroxene]] and, in some varieties, [[olivine]]. Minor amounts of magnetite and ilmenite contribute to its magnetic properties, while trace elements such as nickel and chromium reveal its mantle source."} 
{"id":"occurrence_h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Occurrence"} 
{"id":"occurrence_p","component":"Prose","text":"Basalt covers roughly 70% of the Earth's surface, forming the ocean floor and extensive lava flows like the Deccan Traps and the Columbia River Basalt Group. It also appears on other planetary bodies, notably the lunar mare and the volcanic plains of [[Mars]]."}

