{"id":"root","component":"Column","children":["title","facts","lead","histH","histP","funcH","funcP","signifH","signifP"]} 
{"id":"title","component":"Heading","level":1,"text":"Antikythera Mechanism"} 
{"id":"facts","component":"Facts","items":[{"label":"Date of discovery","value":"1901"},{"label":"Origin","value":"Ancient Greece, ca. 100 BC"},{"label":"Material","value":"Bronze"},{"label":"Purpose","value":"Astronomical calculator"}]} 
{"id":"lead","component":"Prose","text":"The **Antikythera mechanism** is an ancient analog computer recovered from a shipwreck off the Greek island of Antikythera, demonstrating remarkable engineering for its era. It incorporates a complex gear train that models celestial cycles, and its design reflects knowledge of [[lunar eclipse]] prediction, [[Greek astronomy]], and mechanical craftsmanship."} 
{"id":"histH","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"History"} 
{"id":"histP","component":"Prose","text":"Recovered in 1901 by sponge divers, the device sparked scholarly debate about the technological capabilities of the Hellenistic world. Early examinations misidentified it as a simple astrolabe, but X‑ray imaging in the 1970s revealed its intricate gear system, prompting revisions of our understanding of ancient engineering."} 
{"id":"funcH","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Function"} 
{"id":"funcP","component":"Prose","text":"The mechanism contains at least 30 bronze gears that drive dials representing the solar year, the Metonic cycle, and the positions of the known planets. Users could input a date via a hand‑crank and read predictions of eclipses, the phases of the moon, and the timing of the ancient Olympic Games."} 
{"id":"signifH","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Significance"} 
{"id":"signifP","component":"Prose","text":"As the earliest known example of a geared analog computer, the Antikythera mechanism reshapes narratives about the diffusion of scientific knowledge in antiquity. Its existence suggests a tradition of sophisticated instrument making that was lost for centuries, influencing modern reconstructions of ancient technology and inspiring contemporary clockwork design."}

