{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"title","component":"Heading","level":1,"text":"Fermentation"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"facts","component":"Facts","items":[{"label":"Type","value":"Anaerobic metabolic process"},{"label":"Primary Agents","value":"Yeast, bacteria, molds"},{"label":"Products","value":"Alcohol, acids, gases"},{"label":"Historical Use","value":"Thousands of years"},{"label":"Industrial Scale","value":"Food, beverage, biofuel"}]}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"lead","component":"Prose","text":"**Fermentation** is a metabolic conversion of sugars into various compounds by microorganisms, a technique that underpins many foods and drinks worldwide."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"hist_h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Historical Development"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"hist_p","component":"Prose","text":"Earliest evidence of [[bread]] making dates to Neolithic settlements, where wild [[yeast]] leavened dough spontaneously. Ancient [[Egyptians]] refined the process for brewing beer and baking, recognizing the transformative power of microbes long before they were visible. In the 19th century, [[Louis Pasteur]] elucidated the biochemical basis of fermentation, distinguishing it from mere decay and establishing it as a controlled scientific process."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"proc_h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Biochemical Process"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"proc_p","component":"Prose","text":"During fermentation, [[lactic acid bacteria]] convert glucose into lactic acid, lowering pH and preserving food. Simultaneously, [[yeast]] performs alcoholic fermentation, producing ethanol and carbon dioxide, which leavens bread and carbonates beverages. The pathways differ in enzyme sets and end products, yet both operate under anaerobic conditions, regenerating NAD⁺ to sustain glycolysis."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"app_h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Modern Applications"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"app_p","component":"Prose","text":"Today, fermentation drives the production of diverse commodities: [[Kombucha]] tea harnesses symbiotic cultures of bacteria and yeast for a tangy effervescence; biofuel plants ferment corn starch into ethanol for renewable energy; and pharmaceutical firms employ microbial fermentation to synthesize antibiotics and vitamins at scale. The versatility of the process continues to inspire innovative food, fuel, and material solutions."}]}}

text:
{"id":"root","component":"Column","children":["title","facts","lead","historyh","historyp","cultureh","culturep","economyh","economyp"]} 
{"id":"title","component":"Heading","level":1,"text":"Byzantine Empire"} 
{"id":"facts","component":"Facts","items":[{"label":"Capital","value":"Constantinople"},{"label":"Duration","value":"330–1453 AD"},{"label":"Official language","value":"Greek"},{"label":"Religion","value":"Eastern Orthodoxy"}]} 
{"id":"lead","component":"Prose","text":"The **Byzantine Empire** was the continuation of the Roman state in the eastern Mediterranean, renowned for its complex bureaucracy, artistic achievements, and the preservation of classical knowledge."} 
{"id":"historyh","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Historical development"} 
{"id":"historyp","component":"Prose","text":"Founded by Emperor [[Constantine the Great]] in 330 AD, the empire survived the fall of Rome and evolved through crises such as the [[Iconoclasm]] controversy and the sack of [[Constantinople]] in 1204 during the Fourth Crusade, eventually collapsing to the Ottoman Turks in 1453."} 
{"id":"cultureh","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Culture and religion"} 
{"id":"culturep","component":"Prose","text":"Byzantine art blended classical motifs with Christian symbolism, producing iconic mosaics like those in [[Hagia Sophia]], while the codification of canon law under [[Justinian I]] shaped Eastern Orthodox theology for centuries."} 
{"id":"economyh","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Economy and administration"} 
{"id":"economyp","component":"Prose","text":"A sophisticated tax system, state-controlled silk workshops, and a navy based on the [[Greek fire]] incendiary ensured economic vitality, while a hierarchical bureaucracy overseen by the [[Imperial Court]] maintained imperial authority."}

