{"id":"root","component":"Column","children":["title","facts","lead","s1h","s1p","s2h","s2p"]}
{"id":"title","component":"Heading","level":1,"text":"Basalt"}
{"id":"facts","component":"Facts","items":[{"label":"Classification","value":"Extrusive igneous rock"},{"label":"Density","value":"2.8 to 3.2 g/cm³"},{"label":"Color","value":"Dark gray to black"},{"label":"Composition","value":"Mafic (rich in magnesium and iron)"},{"label":"Viscosity","value":"Low (allowing fluid flow)"}]}
{"id":"lead","component":"Prose","text":"**Basalt** is a common extrusive igneous rock of mafic composition, typically described as dark gray or black. It is the most prevalent rock type on the Earth's surface, forming the majority of the oceanic crust and contributing significantly to the continental crust. Basalt forms when magma cools rapidly at or near the surface, resulting in a fine-grained texture."}
{"id":"s1h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Composition and Texture"}
{"id":"s1p","component":"Prose","text":"The mineralogical assemblage of basalt is dominated by [[plagioclase feldspar]] and pyroxene, often accompanied by olivine. The rapid cooling of basaltic magma prevents the growth of large crystals, leading to a fine-grained or aphanitic texture. In some cases, larger crystals known as phenocrysts may be visible, indicating a slower initial cooling phase."}
{"id":"s2h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Geological Occurrence"}
{"id":"s2p","component":"Prose","text":"Basalt is the primary constituent of the [[oceanic crust]] and is responsible for the vast majority of volcanic activity on Earth. It forms extensive flood basalt provinces in continental settings, such as the [[Deccan Traps]] in India. Additionally, basaltic lava flows are a defining feature of [[shield volcanoes]] and mid-ocean ridges, where continuous upwelling of mantle material creates new crust."}

