{"id":"facts","component":"Facts","items":[{"label":"Date","value":"c. 150–100 BCE"},{"label":"Origin","value":"Hellenistic Greece"},{"label":"Function","value":"Astronomical calculator"},{"label":"Material","value":"Bronze"},{"label":"Discovery","value":"1901, Antikythera wreck"}]}
{"id":"lead","component":"Prose","text":"The **Antikythera mechanism** is a preserved ancient Greek hand-powered astronomical device used to predict astronomical positions and eclipses for calendrical and astrological purposes. It is the oldest known example of a sophisticated analogue computer, featuring a complex system of interlocking bronze gears that model the solar and lunar cycles."}
{"id":"s1","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Discovery and Excavation"}
{"id":"s1p","component":"Prose","text":"The device was recovered from the [[Antikythera shipwreck]], a [[Roman]] cargo vessel that sank off the Greek island of Antikythera in the first century BCE. In 1901, divers discovered the corroded lump, which was initially unrecognised as anything but a piece of wreckage. It was not until a decade later that researchers began to appreciate the presence of intricate gearwork hidden within the silt-covered exterior."}
{"id":"s2","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Technical Design"}
{"id":"s2p","component":"Prose","text":"The mechanism contains at least thirty bronze gears, driven by a single crank handle. Its most celebrated feature is a differential gear system that tracks the apparent speed of the [[Moon]] as it orbits [[Earth]]. By calculating the positions of the [[Sun]] and [[Moon]], the device could predict [[eclipse]]s with remarkable accuracy, accounting for the elliptical shape of the lunar orbit."}
{"id":"s3","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Historical Significance"}
{"id":"s3p","component":"Prose","text":"The existence of the [[Antikythera mechanism]] has fundamentally reshaped the history of science. It demonstrates that [[Hellenistic]] engineers possessed a level of mathematical and mechanical sophistication previously thought to have been lost for over a millennium. The device suggests a continuous tradition of advanced engineering that was interrupted by the collapse of the ancient world."}

