text:
{"id":"root","component":"Column","children":["title","facts","lead","s1h","s1p","s2h","s2p","s3h","s3p"]}
{"id":"title","component":"Heading","level":1,"text":"Fermentation"}
{"id":"facts","component":"Facts","items":[{"label":"Type","value":"Anaerobic metabolic process"},{"label":"Key Agents","value":"Bacteria and Yeast"},{"label":"Primary Output","value":"Energy (ATP) and Byproducts"},{"label":"Historical Impact","value":"Foundation of preservation"},{"label":"Modern Use","value":"Biofuel and Pharmaceutical production"}]}
{"id":"lead","component":"Prose","text":"**Fermentation** is a metabolic process in which microorganisms convert carbohydrates into acids, gases, or alcohol. This biochemical pathway occurs in the absence of oxygen and is fundamental to human history, food preservation, and industrial biotechnology. The process allows cells to generate energy when oxidative phosphorylation is unavailable, driving the production of diverse compounds with specific chemical properties."}
{"id":"s1h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Biochemical Mechanism"}
{"id":"s1p","component":"Prose","text":"At the cellular level, fermentation follows glycolysis, a universal metabolic pathway. During this stage, glucose is broken down into pyruvate, yielding a net gain of two ATP molecules. Because the electron transport chain cannot function without oxygen, the cell must regenerate NAD+ to sustain glycolysis. This regeneration is achieved by transferring electrons to organic molecules, such as pyruvate or acetaldehyde, resulting in the formation of specific end products."}
{"id":"s2h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Types and Products"}
{"id":"s2p","component":"Prose","text":"Different microorganisms produce distinct fermentation products. [[Yeast]] primarily performs alcoholic fermentation, converting sugars into ethanol and carbon dioxide, a process central to [[baking]] and beverage production. Bacteria often undergo lactic acid fermentation, reducing pyruvate to lactate, which is critical for [[sourdough]] bread and dairy products. Other pathways include acetate or butyrate production, which have specific applications in industrial chemistry and microbiology."}
{"id":"s3h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Applications and Significance"}
{"id":"s3p","component":"Prose","text":"Fermentation has evolved from a survival technique for food preservation into a cornerstone of modern biotechnology. Today, it is used to produce antibiotics, vaccines, and biofuels. The controlled environment of bioreactors allows scientists to optimize yields of valuable compounds. Understanding the kinetics of fermentation enables the production of sustainable materials and contributes to the development of synthetic biology and metabolic engineering fields."}

