{"id":"root","component":"Column","children":["title","facts","lead","s1h","s1p","s2h","s2p","s3h","s3p"]}
{"id":"title","component":"Heading","level":1,"text":"Byzantine Empire"}
{"id":"facts","component":"Facts","items":[{"label":"Founded","value":"330 AD"},{"label":"Capital","value":"Constantinople"},{"label":"Fall","value":"1453 AD"},{"label":"Official Name","value":"Roman Empire"},{"label":"Final Emperor","value":"Constantine XI Palaiologos"}]}
{"id":"lead","component":"Prose","text":"The **Byzantine Empire** was the eastern continuation of the [[Roman Empire]] that endured for over a millennium, serving as a vital bridge between the ancient world and the [[Renaissance]]. Centered in [[Constantinople]], the empire preserved much of [[Greek]] language, literature, and [[Christianity]], acting as a buffer against Islamic expansion and maintaining complex diplomatic relations with neighboring powers."}]
{"id":"s1h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Origins and Foundation"}
{"id":"s1p","component":"Prose","text":"Emperor [[Constantine the Great]] established [[Constantinople]] as the new capital in 330 AD, shifting the political center of gravity away from [[Rome]]. This strategic location controlled the [[Bosphorus]] strait, granting the empire significant military and economic advantages. The [[Roman Senate]] continued to function, and the imperial title remained [[Augustus]], reflecting the continuity of Roman legal and administrative traditions despite cultural shifts toward Hellenism."}]
{"id":"s2h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Religious and Cultural Identity"}
{"id":"s2p","component":"Prose","text":"The empire became the primary protector of [[Eastern Orthodoxy]], leading to significant theological conflicts such as the [[Iconoclasm]] controversy. Byzantine art, characterized by [[mosaics]] and [[iconography]], exerted profound influence on [[Slavic]] cultures and the [[Russian Empire]]. The [[Greek language]] replaced Latin as the primary administrative tongue, fostering a distinct cultural identity that blended Roman law with Greek philosophy and Christian theology."}]
{"id":"s3h","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Decline and Fall"}
{"id":"s3p","component":"Prose","text":"Internal civil wars and the [[Fourth Crusade]] of 1204 weakened the state, leading to a fragmented period of re-establishment. The rise of the [[Ottoman Empire]] under [[Osman I]] posed an existential threat, culminating in the [[Siege of Constantinople]] in 1453. When [[Mehmed II]] captured the city, the [[Palaiologos]] dynasty ended, marking the definitive close of the Roman imperial tradition and the beginning of a new era in [[Anatolia]]."}

