{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c1","component":"Heading","level":1,"text":"Fermentation"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c2","component":"Facts","items":[{"label":"Definition","value":"biochemical conversion of sugars into acids, gases, or alcohols by microorganisms"},{"label":"Primary agents","value":"yeasts, bacteria, and molds"},{"label":"Historical origin","value":"evidence of fermented foods dates to 7000 BCE"},{"label":"Main products","value":"bread, beer, wine, cheese, yogurt, kimchi, sauerkraut"},{"label":"Key enzymes","value":"invertase, amylase, lactate dehydrogenase, alcohol dehydrogenase"}]}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c3","component":"Prose","text":"Fermentation is a metabolic process in which **Fermentation** transforms carbohydrates into simpler compounds, releasing energy for the microorganisms involved. It occurs under anaerobic or micro‑aerobic conditions and is harnessed by humans for food preservation, flavor development, and the production of alcoholic beverages. The process is driven by enzymes that catalyze the breakdown of sugars, yielding a variety of organic acids, alcohols, and gases."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c4","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Biological mechanisms"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c5","component":"Prose","text":"Fermentation pathways are classified by their end products. Alcoholic fermentation, performed chiefly by *Saccharomyces* yeasts, converts glucose to ethanol and carbon dioxide via glycolysis followed by decarboxylation of pyruvate. Lactic acid fermentation, carried out by lactic acid bacteria such as *Lactobacillus* spp., reduces pyruvate directly to lactic acid, often without gas production. Mixed‑acid fermentation, typical of certain *Enterobacteriaceae*, yields a blend of acids, ethanol, carbon dioxide, and hydrogen. Each pathway regenerates NAD⁺, allowing glycolysis to continue in the absence of oxygen."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c6","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Applications in food and drink"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c7","component":"Prose","text":"The controlled use of fermentation has shaped global cuisines. In baking, yeast leavens dough by producing CO₂, creating the crumb structure of bread. In brewing, yeast metabolism generates ethanol and flavor compounds that define beer and wine styles. Dairy fermentation employs lactic acid bacteria to coagulate milk proteins, producing yogurt, kefir, and various cheeses. Vegetable fermentations, such as kimchi and sauerkraut, rely on lactic acid bacteria to lower pH, inhibiting spoilage microbes and developing characteristic tangy flavors."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c8","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Industrial and biotechnological uses"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c9","component":"Prose","text":"Beyond culinary contexts, fermentation is employed for large‑scale production of biofuels, pharmaceuticals, and biopolymers. Engineered microbial strains can convert renewable feedstocks into ethanol, butanol, or acetone, offering alternatives to petroleum‑based chemicals. Recombinant DNA technology enables the synthesis of insulin, antibiotics, and enzymes through microbial fermentation, making the process a cornerstone of modern biotechnology."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c10","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Health and safety considerations"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c11","component":"Prose","text":"While fermentation can enhance nutritional value—by increasing vitamin content, reducing antinutrients, and adding probiotic microbes—improperly controlled fermentations may produce toxins such as biogenic amines or pathogenic bacteria. Safe practices include maintaining appropriate temperature, pH, and hygiene, and using starter cultures with known safety profiles."}]}}

