{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c1","component":"Heading","level":1,"text":"Basalt"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c2","component":"Facts","items":[{"label":"Type","value":"Igneous rock"},{"label":"Composition","value":"Primarily plagioclase feldspar and pyroxene"},{"label":"Origin","value":"Formed from rapid cooling of mafic lava"},{"label":"Color","value":"Dark gray to black"},{"label":"Density","value":"2.8–3.0 g/cm³"}]}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c3","component":"Prose","text":"Basalt is a fine‑grained **extrusive** igneous rock that constitutes the majority of the [[oceanic crust]] and many [[volcanic islands]]. Its [[rapid cooling]] at or near the Earth's surface prevents the growth of large crystals, giving it a typically uniform, dense texture. The rock is prevalent in both terrestrial and submarine environments, shaping much of the planet's volcanic landscape."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c4","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Formation and Petrology"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c5","component":"Prose","text":"Basalt originates from [[mafic magma]] rich in iron and magnesium. When this magma erupts onto the surface, it cools quickly, forming a glassy or aphanitic texture. In some cases, slow cooling at the base of a lava flow allows the development of phenocrysts, commonly of [[plagioclase]] and clinopyroxene. The mineral assemblage often includes olivine, [[magnetite]], and minor amounts of amphibole. Chemical analyses show a silica content of 45–55 wt %, reflecting its mafic nature."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c6","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Occurrence and Distribution"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c7","component":"Prose","text":"Basaltic flows dominate the ocean floor, creating the layered structure of the basaltic crust that underlies roughly 60 % of Earth's surface. Large continental flood basalts, such as the Deccan Traps in India and the [[Siberian]] Traps in Russia, represent massive eruptive events that can cover millions of square kilometers. Basalt also forms pillow structures when lava extrudes underwater, producing characteristic rounded lobes that solidify rapidly upon contact with seawater."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c8","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Economic and Practical Uses"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c9","component":"Prose","text":"Because of its durability and low porosity, basalt is used as a construction material for road base, concrete aggregate, and architectural stone. Crushed basalt serves as an abrasive in sandblasting and as a raw material for basalt fiber, a high‑strength, fire‑resistant reinforcement comparable to steel. In some regions, basaltic rock is mined for its rich iron content and as a source of volcanic ash for soil amendment."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c10","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Geochemical Significance"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c11","component":"Prose","text":"Basaltic magmas provide insights into mantle composition and dynamics. Trace element patterns and isotopic ratios in basalt reveal variations in mantle source regions, degrees of partial melting, and crustal contamination processes. The widespread distribution of basalt also makes it a key marker in plate tectonic reconstructions, recording the history of seafloor spreading and hotspot activity."}]}}

