{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c1","component":"Heading","level":1,"text":"Antikythera mechanism"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c2","component":"Facts","items":[{"label":"Discovered","value":"1901, Antikythera shipwreck, Greece"},{"label":"Date","value":"circa 100 BC (Late Hellenistic period)"},{"label":"Material","value":"bronze gears, wooden case, corundum inlays"},{"label":"Function","value":"analog computer predicting astronomical cycles"},{"label":"Significance","value":"earliest known complex geared mechanism"}]}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c3","component":"Prose","text":"The **Antikythera mechanism** is an ancient Greek device recovered from a [[Roman-era shipwreck]] off the coast of Antikythera, renowned for its sophisticated system of [[interlocking bronze gears]] that model the motions of the [[Sun, Moon]], and planets. Its intricate construction demonstrates a level of technological mastery previously thought unattainable until the medieval period, providing crucial insight into Hellenistic scientific knowledge and craftsmanship."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c4","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Historical context"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c5","component":"Prose","text":"The mechanism was found among a collection of statues, pottery, and other artifacts that sank around 60 BC, likely en route to Rome. Its existence indicates that Greek scholars such as [[Hipparchus]] and later [[Ptolemy]] possessed not only theoretical astronomy but also practical engineering capabilities. The device reflects the intellectual climate of the late Hellenistic world, where astronomy, mathematics, and mechanics intersected in the work of scholars attached to the [[Library of Alexandria]]."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c6","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Construction and design"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c7","component":"Prose","text":"The core of the mechanism consists of a front and rear plate, each engraved with dials and inscriptions. A series of at least 30 meshed gears transmit rotational motion from a hand-crank to the dials, encoding cycles such as the 19‑year [[Metonic lunar-solar calendar]], the 223‑year [[Saros eclipse series]], and the 18‑year [[Exeligmos correction]]. The front dial displays a solar year, while the rear dial shows a lunar phase indicator with a pointer for the Moon’s age. Additional concentric scales represent the positions of the known planets of the time, using a combination of [[epicyclic gearing]] to approximate their irregular motions."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c8","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Scientific importance"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c9","component":"Prose","text":"The mechanism provides concrete evidence that ancient Greeks applied gear trains to solve astronomical problems, predating similar technology by over a millennium. Its ability to predict solar and lunar eclipses, as well as the timing of the [[Panhellenic Games]], illustrates a practical application of astronomical theory. Modern studies, including [[X‑ray computed tomography]] and [[3D modeling]], have revealed the precise geometry of the gears, confirming the device’s accuracy within a few minutes of actual celestial events."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c10","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Legacy and reconstruction"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c11","component":"Prose","text":"Since its initial discovery, the Antikythera mechanism has inspired numerous [[reconstructions]], both physical and digital, that aim to replicate its functions. These efforts have deepened understanding of ancient engineering, influencing contemporary [[clockwork design]] and prompting reevaluation of the timeline of technological development. The mechanism remains a symbol of the ingenuity of Hellenistic science and a testament to the [[continuity of knowledge]] across centuries."}]}}

