{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c1","component":"Heading","level":1,"text":"Byzantine Empire"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c2","component":"Facts","items":[{"label":"Capital","value":"Constantinople"},{"label":"Duration","value":"330 years (395–1453)"},{"label":"Language","value":"Medieval Greek"},{"label":"Religion","value":"Eastern Orthodoxy"},{"label":"Government","value":"Absolute monarchy"},{"label":"Notable ruler","value":"Justinian I"}]}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c3","component":"Prose","text":"The **Byzantine Empire** emerged from the eastern half of the Roman world, preserving [[Roman law]], administration, and culture while developing a distinct [[Greek‑speaking Christian civilization]] that endured for more than a millennium."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c4","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Foundations and Early Development"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c5","component":"Prose","text":"The empire was formally created in 395 CE when [[Emperor Theodosius I]] divided the Roman Empire between his sons. Its capital, Constantinople, was founded by [[Constantine the Great]] in 330 CE on the site of ancient Byzantium. The city’s strategic location on the [[Bosporus]] controlled key trade routes between Europe and Asia, providing the new state with economic and military advantages that shaped its early expansion."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c6","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Justinian’s Reign and Legal Legacy"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c7","component":"Prose","text":"Emperor [[Justinian I]] (527–565) sought to restore the Roman Empire’s former glory. He launched ambitious military campaigns that temporarily reconquered parts of Italy, North Africa, and Spain. Justinian’s most enduring contribution was the codification of Roman law in the [[Corpus Juris Civilis]], a systematic collection that became the foundation of civil law in many modern jurisdictions. His building program, exemplified by the [[Hagia Sophia]], blended classical engineering with innovative dome construction, influencing Byzantine architecture for centuries."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c8","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Religious and Cultural Identity"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c9","component":"Prose","text":"[[Eastern Orthodoxy]] defined the empire’s religious life, distinguishing it from the Latin West. Theological disputes, such as the [[Iconoclast controversy]] of the 8th–9th centuries, tested the relationship between church and state but ultimately reinforced the veneration of icons. [[Byzantine art]], characterized by mosaics, illuminated manuscripts, and iconography, reflected a synthesis of classical motifs and Christian symbolism, spreading through trade and missionary activity to Slavic lands and beyond."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c10","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Decline and Fall"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c11","component":"Prose","text":"From the 11th century onward, the empire faced mounting pressures: [[Seljuk Turkish incursions]], the [[Crusader sack of Constantinople]] in 1204, and the rise of the Ottoman Turks. Despite occasional revivals, the loss of key territories eroded imperial resources. The final blow came on 29 May 1453, when Ottoman forces under [[Sultan Mehmed II]] captured Constantinople, marking the end of the Byzantine state and the transition of its legacy into the Ottoman realm."}]}}

