{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c1","component":"Heading","level":1,"text":"Antikythera mechanism"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c2","component":"Facts","items":[{"label":"Discovered","value":"1901"},{"label":"Location","value":"Antikythera, Greece"},{"label":"Date","value":"c. 150–100 BCE"},{"label":"Material","value":"Bronze"},{"label":"Function","value":"Astronomical calculator"}]}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c3","component":"Prose","text":"The **Antikythera mechanism** is a [[hand-powered analog computer]] designed to calculate astronomical positions. It is the oldest known surviving example of a complex mechanical device, featuring a sophisticated system of gears that predicted [[solar and lunar eclipses]]. The artifact was recovered from a shipwreck off the [[Greek island of Antikythera]] in 1901, where it had remained submerged for nearly two millennia. Its discovery revealed that ancient Greek engineers possessed a level of technological sophistication previously thought impossible for the classical period."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c4","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Construction and Design"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c5","component":"Prose","text":"The mechanism consists of at least [[thirty bronze gears]], interlocked to track the movements of the Sun and Moon. These gears are housed within a [[wooden box]], the contents of which are now lost, leaving only the corroded bronze fragments. The central gear train drives a complex system of epicycles, allowing the device to model the [[elliptical orbit of the Moon]]. This mathematical precision indicates that the builder understood the varying speed of the Moon as it moved through its orbit, a concept that would not be widely accepted in Western astronomy for another millennium."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c6","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Functionality and Accuracy"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c7","component":"Prose","text":"The device served as a sophisticated orrery, predicting the positions of the Sun, Moon, and potentially the [[five known planets]]. It included a calendar system based on the 19-year [[Metonic cycle]], which aligned lunar months with solar years. The mechanism could predict solar and lunar eclipses with high accuracy, calculating their timing and location. Most remarkably, it included a separate dial for the [[Olympic Games]], linking celestial events with the Panhellenic festival cycle. This integration of astronomy and civic life suggests the device was used for practical navigation and religious scheduling."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c8","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Historical Significance"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c9","component":"Prose","text":"For centuries, scholars believed that such complex mechanical devices did not exist in the ancient world. The existence of the Antikythera mechanism contradicts the notion that ancient technology was primitive. Recent imaging techniques, including [[X-ray tomography]], have revealed the intricate details of the [[gear teeth]] and the inscriptions on the casing. These findings confirm that the device was a [[mass-produced item]], as similar mechanisms have been identified in other archaeological contexts. The discovery has fundamentally altered the understanding of Greek science, demonstrating a sophisticated grasp of mechanical engineering and mathematical astronomy."}]}}

