{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c1","component":"Heading","level":1,"text":"Fermentation"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c2","component":"Facts","items":[{"label":"Definition","value":"Metabolic conversion of sugar to acids, gases, or alcohol"},{"label":"Catalyst","value":"Enzymes produced by microorganisms"},{"label":"Energy Yield","value":"Low energy extraction per glucose molecule"},{"label":"Oxygen Requirement","value":"Anaerobic process"},{"label":"Historical Role","value":"Foundation of food preservation and beverage production"}]}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c3","component":"Prose","text":"**Fermentation** is a metabolic process in which molecules such as [[glucose]] are partially oxidized or reduced to produce energy in the absence of oxygen. Unlike [[aerobic respiration]], which completely breaks down glucose to carbon dioxide and water, fermentation yields significantly less ATP but allows cells to regenerate [[NAD+]] so that glycolysis can continue. This biochemical pathway is primarily driven by microorganisms, including bacteria, [[yeast]], and fungi, which secrete specific enzymes to catalyze the transformation of organic substrates into various end products."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c4","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Biochemical Mechanisms"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c5","component":"Prose","text":"The core pathway of most fermentations is glycolysis, where one molecule of glucose is converted into two molecules of [[pyruvate]]. Under anaerobic conditions, pyruvate cannot enter the citric acid cycle. Instead, it undergoes further reduction to maintain the redox balance within the cell. The specific end product depends on the organism and the pathway utilized. In yeast and many plant cells, pyruvate is decarboxylated to [[acetaldehyde]] and then reduced to ethanol and carbon dioxide. In [[lactic acid]] bacteria and animal muscle cells, pyruvate is directly reduced to lactic acid. These reactions are reversible and highly sensitive to pH and temperature, requiring precise control to optimize yield and prevent contamination by competing microbial species."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c6","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Industrial and Culinary Applications"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c7","component":"Prose","text":"Fermentation has been harnessed by humans for millennia to preserve food and create complex flavors. In the dairy industry, [[lactic acid bacteria]] convert [[lactose]] into lactic acid, lowering the pH and coagulating proteins to produce yogurt, cheese, and sour cream. In baking, yeast ferments sugars to produce [[carbon dioxide]] gas, causing dough to rise, and ethanol, which evaporates during baking. The beverage industry relies heavily on fermentation to produce [[beer]], wine, and spirits, where specific strains of yeast are selected for their flavor profiles and alcohol tolerance."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c8","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Industrial and Biotechnological Uses"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c9","component":"Prose","text":"Beyond food, fermentation is a cornerstone of the bioeconomy. It is used to produce industrial chemicals such as [[acetone]], butanol, and citric acid. In biotechnology, engineered microorganisms are used to synthesize pharmaceuticals, including [[insulin]] and vaccines, as well as biofuels. The scalability of fermentation processes allows for the mass production of these compounds in controlled bioreactors, offering a sustainable alternative to petrochemical synthesis. The versatility of microbial metabolism continues to drive innovation in green chemistry and sustainable manufacturing."}]}}

