{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c1","component":"Heading","level":1,"text":"Byzantine Empire"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c2","component":"Facts","items":[{"label":"Capital","value":"Constantinople"},{"label":"Founding","value":"330 AD"},{"label":"Dissolution","value":"1453 AD"},{"label":"Language","value":"Greek"},{"label":"Religion","value":"Eastern Orthodox Christianity"}]}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c3","component":"Prose","text":"The **Byzantine Empire** was the continuation of the [[Roman Empire]] in its [[Eastern provinces]], lasting for nearly a millennium after the division of the Roman state. It served as a bridge between [[Classical antiquity]] and the [[medieval era]], preserving Greek and Roman legal, philosophical, and artistic traditions while developing a distinct imperial identity centered on the city of Constantinople."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c4","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Founding and Early History"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c5","component":"Prose","text":"[[Emperor Constantine the Great]] established Constantinople as the new eastern capital in 330 AD. The city’s strategic location at the [[Bosphorus strait]] allowed the empire to control vital trade routes between Europe and Asia. Following the formal split of the empire in 395, the east retained a larger population and more stable finances than the western half, which eventually collapsed under [[Germanic invasions]]."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c6","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Political Structure and Law"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c7","component":"Prose","text":"The Byzantine state was characterized by a highly centralized autocracy, where the emperor held both civil and religious authority. [[Emperor Justinian I]] codified Roman law in the [[Corpus Juris Civilis]], a comprehensive legal collection that influenced European legal systems for centuries. The [[bureaucratic structure]] was complex, designed to extract maximum revenue while maintaining order in a diverse and often hostile neighborhood."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c8","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Religion and Culture"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c9","component":"Prose","text":"[[Eastern Orthodox Christianity]] became the state religion, deeply intertwining faith with imperial politics. The [[Hagia Sophia]], built under Justinian, stood as a symbol of imperial power and architectural mastery. Byzantine culture emphasized [[religious iconography]], intricate mosaics, and manuscript illumination. The empire also played a crucial role in preserving ancient Greek texts, which later fueled the European Renaissance."}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c10","component":"Heading","level":2,"text":"Decline and Fall"}]}}
{"version":"1.0","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"page","components":[{"id":"c11","component":"Prose","text":"The empire faced persistent threats from the [[Slavs]], [[Bulgars]], Arabs, and Seljuk Turks. The [[Fourth Crusade]] in 1204 shattered imperial cohesion when Venetian and French forces sacked Constantinople, leading to a fragmented period of competing states. The Ottoman Turks eventually besieged the capital, and in 1453, [[Sultan Mehmed II]] conquered the city, marking the end of the Byzantine Empire. Its legacy persisted in the Russian Tsardom, which claimed to be its spiritual successor."}]}}
